Chronic Workplace Microaggression Burden - Adjustment Disorder - V62.29 (Other problem related to employment)
Diversity Equity and Inclusion
Anticipatory workplace dread, Suppressed emotional expression, Tension headaches

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: DEI-006
Reason for Visit: Acute escalation of physical distress characterized by an intractable headache and severe anticipatory anxiety, leading to functional incapacitation.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted an acute, severe headache accompanied by localized jaw pain and systemic tension, requiring urgent assessment to rule out hypertensive emergency or acute neurologic event prior to the next available in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: My head feels like it's in a vice, my jaw is locked, and the thought of going to work tomorrow makes me want to vomit.
History of Present Illness: The patient reports a progressive history of dread and anticipatory anxiety specifically tied to the beginning of the work week. This involves compulsive rumination on ambiguous interactions with colleagues and suppressed emotional expression at work to avoid negative stereotypes. Over the past 72 hours, this stress manifested as severe physical symptoms, including an unrelenting tension headache and severe bruxism leading to acute jaw pain. The patient describes impaired executive functioning and focus, progressive emotional detachment, frequent use of sick leave unlinked to infectious illness, feelings of intense isolation despite working in open-plan environments, and chronic self-doubt regarding professional competency despite objective success.
Functional Status: Independent, but currently unable to concentrate or rest due to severe cranial and facial pain.
Social/Safety Concerns: Ongoing exposure to a toxic professional environment with no immediate exit strategy.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 148/92 | HR: 95 | Temp: 36.7 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 82 Kg
General: Appears tense, uncomfortable, frequently rubbing temples and masseter muscles.
Physical Exam: Cardiovascular: Mildly tachycardic, regular rhythm, no murmurs. Respiratory: Clear bilaterally. Neurological: Cranial nerves intact. No focal deficits. Musculoskeletal: Severe, symmetrical tenderness over bilateral temporalis, masseter, and occipital muscle groups. Significant restricted mouth opening secondary to spasm. Anticipatory workplace dread is evident during the interview, along with an initial suppressed emotional expression that gave way to tearfulness when the diagnosis was discussed.
Environmental Observations: Residence is neat, but the patient's work laptop is open and active on the dining table.
Medication Reconciliation: Taking paracetamol and ibuprofen regularly for the past 48 hours without relief.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Chronic Workplace Microaggression Burden - Adjustment Disorder - V62.29 (Other problem related to employment) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Subarachnoid Hemorrhage - Ruled out/unlikely due to the gradual onset over days, bilateral muscular distribution, and absence of meningism or photophobia.

Temporal Arteritis - Ruled out/unlikely due to patient age, bilateral nature of the pain, normal visual acuity, and direct correlation with masseter spasm (bruxism).

Primary Trigeminal Neuralgia - Ruled out/unlikely due to the continuous, aching nature of the pain rather than brief, sharp, electric-shock sensations.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with an acute exacerbation of somatic symptoms (intractable tension headache, masseter spasm) and elevated blood pressure (148/92) directly triggered by chronic occupational stress and microaggressions. Immediate face-to-face assessment was necessary to physically examine the cranial nerves and definitively exclude a dangerous secondary headache disorder (such as an intracranial event or hypertensive crisis). Delaying care until routine hours would have prolonged severe pain, increased the risk of dental injury from bruxism, and compounded the psychological crisis, thereby fulfilling the criteria for an urgent after-hours intervention.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered oral diazepam 5mg to break the acute cycle of bruxism and muscle spasm. Prescribed a short course of naproxen 500mg twice daily with food. Recommended routine GP review of work-specific blood pressure readings and salivary secretory IgA.
Positive Behavioral Change: To manage the bruxism, implement a 5-minute jaw relaxation protocol (gentle opening against resistance) before sleep; tracking a reduction in morning jaw pain will reinforce compliance. To mitigate anticipatory dread, enforce a strict disconnection from work communications by physically closing and storing the laptop at 6 PM; substituting this time with a neutral, engaging hobby will create a psychological barrier between work and home.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Referral to employee assistance program (EAP) or independent occupational psychology services.
DME/Safety (Optional): Recommend a dental review for a fitted occlusal splint (night guard).
Follow-up: Routine GP review within 72 hours for blood pressure check, pain management review, and consideration of a medical certificate for stress leave.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate presentation to the Emergency Department or contact 000 if experiencing sudden, explosive worsening of the headache ("thunderclap"), visual changes, limb weakness, or difficulty speaking.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive pending investigations; conditions are dynamic and require ongoing review.
Clinical Course: The acute muscle spasm and headache should improve over 3 to 5 days with muscle relaxants and NSAIDs. Long-term resolution requires addressing the occupational environment.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek emergency medical care immediately if your headache suddenly becomes the worst of your life, if you develop a fever and stiff neck, or if you lose vision or strength in any part of your body.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The pain is caused by sustained, unconscious clenching of your jaw and scalp muscles due to severe stress. The medication will relax these muscles and reduce the inflammation built up over the last few days.
Actionable Steps: Take the anti-inflammatory medication with food to protect your stomach. Physically put your work laptop out of sight to signal to your brain that the workday is over.

